        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-10T15:52:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Good point.  The hard reset is actually occurring before we enter the TypeCSnkAttached state, which is valid because the PDC is trying establish a PD connection.\n\nI\u0027ll change the logic of `hard_reset_sent` to check the connection state and SNK path state before setting this flag.",
      "parentUuid": "a975c4e4_1d5ac52c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1947,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 1952,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "992304a33b4cd913805c5231f68146c343eac636",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "39c591c5_2837c93d",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/pd_controller/pdc_power_mgmt.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1952,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-10T16:52:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To clarify my comment. The hard reset isn\u0027t real. It\u0027s more the case that we aren\u0027t correctly handling the status change bits. Bit 7 (PD Reset Complete) to me implies that there was a hard reset and then a USB-PD connection has been established again.\n\nHard reset is a special k-code that can only be sent/detected by a USB-PD capable partner. In TCPMv2, hard reset is sent to a port partner that isn\u0027t responding to SRC CAPs if in a source power role, and similarly if SRC CAPs aren\u0027t received in a SNK power role. Even if the PDC was doing something similar, if it worked, then connector status would change showing that it\u0027s a USB-PD connection.",
      "parentUuid": "9973eeda_35420be4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1947,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 1952,
        "endChar": 49
      },
